<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zara Yaqob</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1434">1434</a>–<a href="/years/1468">1468</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a> Yaqob was Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> from 1434 to 1468, one of the most powerful rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>. He centralised royal authority, reformed the Ethiopian Church and promoted religious literature. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> treat his reign as the medieval peak of the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob.jpg" alt="Zara Yaqob" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown painter 17th century · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zara_Yacob.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zara Yaqob, born in 1399 at Telq in the province of Fatagar, belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> people and was the youngest son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawit-i">Dawit I</a> and his wife Igzi Kebra. A tradition repeated by Paul B.</p><p>Henze says that the jealousy of his brother Emperor Tewodros I led courtiers to raise him in secret in Tigray, but Taddesse Tamrat calls this very improbable in its details, noting that Zara Yaqob himself wrote that he was brought down from the royal prison of Mount Gishan only on the eve of his accession. After Dawit's death Tewodros had him confined on Amba Geshen, around 1414. His supporters kept him a candidate, and the quick succession of his older brothers left him the eldest qualified claimant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He became emperor in 1434 under the regnal name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> but was crowned only in 1436, at Axum. Early in his reign he campaigned against surviving pagan worship. According to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1784, spies denounced heretics, who were beheaded in public, and several of his own children were executed as heretics.</p><p>He ordered every Christian to wear fillets bearing devotional inscriptions, and those who refused were looted and beaten or executed. He also settled a century-old dispute over Sabbath observance by a compromise between the Egyptian Abuna Mikael and Gabriel and the followers of Ewostatewos, which the Ewostathians accepted by 1442 and the Abuns at the Council of Debre Mitmaq in 1450.</p><p>Abroad he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badlay">Badlay</a> ad-Din, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Adal, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gomit">Battle of Gomit</a> in 1445, consolidating his hold over the Sidamo kingdoms of the south. He invaded Hadiya for unpaid tribute and married its princess Eleni. In the north he settled warriors from Shewa in Hamassien and, in 1464 or 1465, pillaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> and the Dahlak archipelago. After a bright light in 1456, which most historians identify as Halley's Comet, he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-berhan">Debre Berhan</a> his capital and built churches and a palace there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zara Yaqob died on 26 August 1468 after a reign of 34 years and 2 months. He is known for the Geʽez literature that flourished under him. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ullendorff">Edward Ullendorff</a> called him a ruler unequalled since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana">Ezana</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> the only later emperors comparable. David Buxton noted that his years of seclusion left him ferocious and, late in life, a lonely man isolated by suspicion. In the sixteenth century Ahmed ibn Ibrahim al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> ordered his palace at Debre Birhan destroyed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zara Yaqob” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The defeat and death of Sultan Badlay at the Battle of Gomit in 1445, against Emperor <b>Zara Yaqob</b>, showed this limitation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The medieval peak came under <b>Zara Yaqob</b>, who reigned from 1434 to 1468.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor <b>Zara Yaqob</b> himself wrote the Book of Light, and the Fetha Negest, “Laws of the Kings”, translated around 1450, was Ethiopia’s supreme law until 1931.</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations were tense: Garad Mahiko rebelled against <b>Zara Yaqob</b> in the 15th century, while a Hadiya princess, Eleni, married the emperor and became a powerful empress dowager.</blockquote><a href="/state/hadiya-sultanate">Hadiya Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The office gained strength in the 15th century under <b>Zara Yaqob</b>, and its seat became Debarwa.</blockquote><a href="/state/medri-bahri">Medri Bahri</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-berhan">Debre Berhan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.68,39.53&amp;year=1451" aria-label="Debre Berhan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5641 1802 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Zara Yaqob"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 2072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 2072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=9.68,39.53&amp;year=1451">What was here in 1451? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
